1
00:00:04,411 --> 00:00:27,865
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, another way to do a guided contemplation in a way that matters, towards developing wisdom at least, would be to ponder on various mental states that usually are something that people don't want to think about.

2
00:00:28,526 --> 00:00:30,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something disagreeable.

3
00:00:31,742 --> 00:00:48,414
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the contemplation of death in the Buddhist practice is one of the... well, the quickest, the sharpest, obviously, a bit risky, ways of developing wisdom and abandoning, experiencing dispassion towards existence.

4
00:00:48,454 --> 00:00:59,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, there are other ways as well that you could... other themes that you can dwell on, that can help you develop the same dispassion.

5
00:01:00,683 --> 00:01:04,944
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not enough to think about it once or twice, just have a thought or something.

6
00:01:05,784 --> 00:01:07,465
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, on some occasion, I suppose, yeah.

7
00:01:07,825 --> 00:01:18,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if the conditions are right, so to speak, you're in the right frame of mind, one thought is enough to establish a theme that will have a deeper emotional significance on you.

8
00:01:18,947 --> 00:01:21,048
[SPEAKER_00]: But then this won't necessarily last.

9
00:01:21,863 --> 00:01:31,007
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're only establishing your practice of contemplation upon that feeling of quick inspiration, then you won't be really doing the work.

10
00:01:31,647 --> 00:01:38,650
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be able to think and ponder on things, even if you don't necessarily feel like doing so.

11
00:01:39,091 --> 00:01:46,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this particular instance, the theme you could be developing, for example, is the sense of safety.

12
00:01:46,214 --> 00:01:48,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, just whatever situation you're living in,

13
00:01:50,203 --> 00:01:53,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Be it a monk, an ordained person, or a lay person, or whatever.

14
00:01:54,445 --> 00:01:58,927
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense of safety, which is obviously inseparable from your existential sense of self.

15
00:01:59,568 --> 00:02:05,091
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to, well, start looking into it, to begin with.

16
00:02:07,272 --> 00:02:09,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself, safety, safety.

17
00:02:09,513 --> 00:02:10,573
[SPEAKER_00]: What does safety mean for me?

18
00:02:10,753 --> 00:02:11,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I feel safe now?

19
00:02:14,252 --> 00:02:20,196
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is yes, which probably most people would answer yes, then ask yourself a further question.

20
00:02:20,697 --> 00:02:25,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that sense of safety something I can always have a say in?

21
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:27,901
[SPEAKER_00]: I can always be in charge of?

22
00:02:29,062 --> 00:02:36,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it sort of I can only kind of... my efforts can only approximate that result of safety, as in I hope for the best?

23
00:02:39,765 --> 00:02:45,771
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, as I mentioned many times in other talks, you don't want to be, like, just asking questions, providing answers.

24
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:52,337
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to ask the question, upon safety in this case, that will then make you think about how you feel.

25
00:02:52,797 --> 00:02:54,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, what does that safety mean for you?

26
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:57,282
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what does the safety mean for you?

27
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:05,870
[SPEAKER_00]: You can let that question endure and think about it.

28
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:07,091
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no right or wrong answer.

29
00:03:07,131 --> 00:03:09,031
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really just clarify what's already there.

30
00:03:09,071 --> 00:03:10,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of safety that you're talking about.

31
00:03:11,391 --> 00:03:14,752
[SPEAKER_00]: But what does that mean?

32
00:03:14,792 --> 00:03:17,453
[SPEAKER_00]: What constitutes safety in your experience?

33
00:03:21,853 --> 00:03:23,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that absence of physical discomfort?

34
00:03:24,234 --> 00:03:26,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Of physical threat?

35
00:03:26,934 --> 00:03:28,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotional absence of emotional discomfort?

36
00:03:29,835 --> 00:03:30,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Both?

37
00:03:32,247 --> 00:03:33,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, that's probably it.

38
00:03:33,988 --> 00:03:35,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But then ask yourself, so what is then in common?

39
00:03:37,831 --> 00:03:45,856
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a common thread through... to physical discomfort, emotional discomfort, mental discomfort?

40
00:03:46,136 --> 00:03:50,699
[SPEAKER_00]: What is... absence of it means safety.

41
00:03:50,719 --> 00:03:57,743
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the common absence in these phenomena that determine your sense of current safety?

42
00:04:00,337 --> 00:04:06,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a absence of unpleasant feeling on that fundamental existential level.

43
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:14,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, you can be experiencing some annoyance, but fundamentally that doesn't compromise your sense of safety.

44
00:04:15,042 --> 00:04:17,363
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you want to be dwelling on the sense of safety.

45
00:04:18,863 --> 00:04:20,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it not compromised?

46
00:04:20,884 --> 00:04:22,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it because you have a say in it?

47
00:04:23,105 --> 00:04:23,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you feel safe?

48
00:04:24,625 --> 00:04:28,847
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, your heart can stop any, like, next second, and that's it.

49
00:04:28,927 --> 00:04:29,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Your safety is gone.

50
00:04:30,813 --> 00:04:33,294
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, your input in your own safety is very relative.

51
00:04:34,575 --> 00:04:39,737
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what an ignorant mind, the mind that's not devoted to training, that's what it forgets.

52
00:04:40,897 --> 00:04:48,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody knows that you're fundamentally not in control, but everybody chooses to not dwell on it, because it's too unpleasant.

53
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:52,302
[SPEAKER_00]: It brings anxiety, dread, discomfort, all sorts of things.

54
00:04:52,962 --> 00:04:59,625
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the ignorance, that's avijja, choosing to not think about certain things that pertain to the nature of existence.

55
00:05:02,963 --> 00:05:12,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you then start choosing against that grain of avoiding thinking about things that cause me anxiety, eh... that's how you start... you stop ignoring it.

56
00:05:12,885 --> 00:05:24,988
[SPEAKER_00]: By... by... with the absence of ignorance, the attitude of ignorance, you will... you will become more and more familiar with the nature of those phenomena, which means, eh... you will be less and less passionate about life.

57
00:05:25,529 --> 00:05:26,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Life as you know it.

58
00:05:27,469 --> 00:05:30,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Because life as you know it depends upon ignorance of certain things.

59
00:05:31,732 --> 00:05:44,154
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the mind that's devoted to ignorance is the mind that finds joy in sensuality, that finds justification in anger, that finds peace in distraction.

60
00:05:46,255 --> 00:05:56,576
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you uproot the ignorance, none of these three things can be manifested then, or can continue their uninterrupted manifestation in your life.

61
00:05:56,616 --> 00:06:00,197
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you'll still be alive, but now the life will have a different value for you.

62
00:06:01,888 --> 00:06:03,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sense of safety.

63
00:06:03,188 --> 00:06:16,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it's something quite fundamental, very important to all of us, but something that pretty much everyone takes for granted, until it's too late, usually.

64
00:06:17,173 --> 00:06:18,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't want to wait for that.

65
00:06:18,614 --> 00:06:26,416
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to start questioning it and challenging it, while you still can, while you still can make a choice about it.

66
00:06:28,997 --> 00:06:30,178
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sense of safety means

67
00:06:31,837 --> 00:06:38,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of displeasure, threatening, things that threaten my life, but... or... or the life I'm used to it.

68
00:06:39,463 --> 00:06:48,711
[SPEAKER_00]: See, like, for a person who is fully devoted and engaged in sensuality, not having access to sensual objects is already feel... feels like a threat, existential threat.

69
00:06:49,972 --> 00:07:00,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody who is withdrawn from sensuality, doesn't devote his time just... just with sensual thoughts and actions, he won't feel threatened, if he has no access to that which he is indifferent towards.

70
00:07:01,770 --> 00:07:04,774
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, he might feel threatened towards something that he does depend on.

71
00:07:05,675 --> 00:07:06,556
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like in the suttas.

72
00:07:06,737 --> 00:07:08,679
[SPEAKER_00]: We discussed it before as well.

73
00:07:10,562 --> 00:07:14,887
[SPEAKER_00]: When the monk dwells in the forest, he develops his mind upon the perceptions of the forest.

74
00:07:15,027 --> 00:07:17,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when he goes to the village, nothing in the village.

75
00:07:17,673 --> 00:07:25,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, nothing that he sees, smells, tastes, touches, da-da-da, in the village, can disturb his mind, which is established upon the perceptions of the forest.

76
00:07:25,683 --> 00:07:30,049
[SPEAKER_00]: But then the Buddha says, if something were to happen to that forest, his mind would be disturbed.

77
00:07:30,389 --> 00:07:32,912
[SPEAKER_00]: So then he sees that, and then he goes a step higher.

78
00:07:33,653 --> 00:07:34,934
[SPEAKER_00]: What is that forest then?

79
00:07:35,094 --> 00:07:36,135
[SPEAKER_00]: What is higher in the forest?

80
00:07:36,195 --> 00:07:41,500
[SPEAKER_00]: It's perception of earth, or you can even say perception of life, or perception of the living body, mindfulness of the body.

81
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:57,134
[SPEAKER_00]: You establish your mind upon that, then doesn't matter the circumstance, doesn't matter the change of the circumstances, whether it's adverse or positive or whatever, you would remain unaffected, because your mind has not been cultivating dependence upon those things.

82
00:07:58,140 --> 00:08:00,262
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you want to start questioning the sense of safety.

83
00:08:01,182 --> 00:08:07,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, for most people, the starting point is that that sense of safety is proliferated too far out.

84
00:08:07,948 --> 00:08:11,931
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, it's not just your health and your life that you depend on.

85
00:08:12,592 --> 00:08:20,518
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your health, your life, your work, your family, your friends, people you want to do what you tell them to do.

86
00:08:21,528 --> 00:08:28,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Access to food, access to distraction, access to sensual objects, that's where people's minds are, that's how far out.

87
00:08:29,171 --> 00:08:34,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any of those things, anything less than those things, they would be experiencing a degree of threat.

88
00:08:36,014 --> 00:08:37,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Like threat to the lifestyle that I know.

89
00:08:38,875 --> 00:08:42,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, which is more correctly to say, threat to the lifestyle that I take for granted and assume.

90
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:49,700
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to reflect that those things will inevitably, they have to be taken away from you.

91
00:08:50,573 --> 00:09:06,905
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you better start dwelling on that before it's too late, rather than waiting for them to be taken away from you and then you think somehow... Because, if you haven't been dealing with those things while they were in front of you, what makes you think you'll be able to deal with them when they're removed, against your will?

92
00:09:07,385 --> 00:09:07,866
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too late.

93
00:09:09,607 --> 00:09:14,611
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like being able to withhold, I don't know, 300 kilos on your back, suddenly.

94
00:09:14,651 --> 00:09:17,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Never ever made the effort to put weight on your back and endure it,

95
00:09:18,546 --> 00:09:20,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Just hope that when it comes, you'll be able to hold it.

96
00:09:21,227 --> 00:09:23,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's quite a foolish attitude.

97
00:09:25,027 --> 00:09:32,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your meditation, your contemplation can be reflecting upon how far is your sense of safety proliferated?

98
00:09:34,229 --> 00:09:40,311
[SPEAKER_00]: How much did you take for granted, basically, of your life, of your sense experience, of people around you?

99
00:09:40,851 --> 00:09:42,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Hey, quiet!

100
00:09:44,371 --> 00:09:45,652
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, start reining it in.

101
00:09:48,884 --> 00:09:55,649
[SPEAKER_00]: You're bringing your mind back to things that are not so much out of your control.

102
00:10:00,753 --> 00:10:02,415
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can ask, where is my sense of safety?

103
00:10:02,455 --> 00:10:06,538
[SPEAKER_00]: What makes me feel safe right here, right now?

104
00:10:06,778 --> 00:10:11,282
[SPEAKER_00]: If I were to get sick, would I experience anxiety?

105
00:10:11,502 --> 00:10:11,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

106
00:10:12,683 --> 00:10:17,147
[SPEAKER_00]: That means then that there is a degree of threat in sickness, which means

107
00:10:18,374 --> 00:10:30,336
[SPEAKER_00]: My safety is overreaching, my sense of safety, because you can only do so much to control, to have input in your health, but fundamentally it's not on you.

108
00:10:31,096 --> 00:10:41,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it doesn't mean you neglect your health, but don't use the amount of input you have in your health, don't use that to develop a notion that you are in charge of your health.

109
00:10:42,358 --> 00:10:45,019
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't ignore the fact that you can never ever

110
00:10:45,606 --> 00:10:48,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Step outside of it and become the full, complete master.

111
00:10:50,129 --> 00:10:53,251
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you have any doubt about that, just ask... just imagine getting sick.

112
00:10:53,391 --> 00:10:54,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be affected?

113
00:10:54,852 --> 00:10:57,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the answer is yes, emotionally, mentally, I would be affected.

114
00:10:58,514 --> 00:11:04,677
[SPEAKER_00]: I would, you know, be afraid or whatever, depending on what sickness it is, means I am threatened by it.

115
00:11:04,957 --> 00:11:12,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And if there is... if there is an experience of threat, can only affect you, because your assumption is

116
00:11:13,913 --> 00:11:16,315
[SPEAKER_00]: It's taking up things that are not in your control.

117
00:11:16,915 --> 00:11:17,295
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

118
00:11:18,997 --> 00:11:28,383
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stop taking things that don't belong to you, including your own health, your own body, your own life and everything else that comes out of it, nothing can threaten you.

119
00:11:28,403 --> 00:11:30,665
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no basis for that threat to land.

120
00:11:33,126 --> 00:11:35,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could see, nothing has changed in things.

121
00:11:35,288 --> 00:11:35,948
[SPEAKER_00]: You still have your life.

122
00:11:35,968 --> 00:11:36,549
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still alive.

123
00:11:36,609 --> 00:11:37,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahant is still alive.

124
00:11:38,110 --> 00:11:42,773
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nothing has changed, yet everything has changed because your attitude has changed.

125
00:11:43,688 --> 00:11:46,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need to rewrite the significance of things.

126
00:11:46,911 --> 00:11:50,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, liability to illness stays for Puthujjana and for an arahant.

127
00:11:51,114 --> 00:12:00,822
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just because Puthujjana is taking his life for granted, his senses, his functioning senses as fully mine, then he will be affected when illness comes.

128
00:12:01,342 --> 00:12:05,825
[SPEAKER_00]: But arahant has removed his attitude, not the senses or their liability and everything else.

129
00:12:06,326 --> 00:12:08,508
[SPEAKER_00]: He has just removed his assumption of ownership.

130
00:12:09,108 --> 00:12:11,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why he's... he has no fear.

131
00:12:11,310 --> 00:12:12,751
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot be threatened by anything.

132
00:12:16,134 --> 00:12:33,706
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, on a practical level, you can... if you dwell, for example, on... on the nature of your body, nature of your life, mindfulness of death, means you are then establishing your mind upon something that's... that's better than... than depending or trying to control the circumstances.

133
00:12:33,766 --> 00:12:38,109
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, okay, I don't care what happens over there, but I care what happens in my house.

134
00:12:38,489 --> 00:12:40,170
[SPEAKER_00]: I care what happens to my house.

135
00:12:40,970 --> 00:12:44,633
[SPEAKER_00]: I care to who moves and becomes my neighbor or all these things.

136
00:12:46,193 --> 00:13:03,557
[SPEAKER_00]: But if all you care, even when you're in your house, is about mindfulness of the body, mindfulness of liability to death or sickness and so on, even if things happen to your house, to your own bedroom, to your own, like, environment, the closest environment, you won't be affected by it.

137
00:13:04,377 --> 00:13:08,578
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you won't be affected even if your body starts to break apart while you're still alive.

138
00:13:09,739 --> 00:13:14,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your mind has already withdrawn from dependence on the senses that were secondary to the body.

139
00:13:16,638 --> 00:13:21,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's something, again, it's not something you would get instantaneously, like one thought and that's it, everything is clear.

140
00:13:22,061 --> 00:13:27,885
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically something you have to keep doing, because it's not about... Again, all these truths are, in a way, obvious.

141
00:13:29,046 --> 00:13:29,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not the problem.

142
00:13:30,407 --> 00:13:36,070
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that your actions, your choices, your intentions, are choosing to ignore them.

143
00:13:36,971 --> 00:13:37,331
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

144
00:13:38,232 --> 00:13:40,153
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep repeating Avijja.

145
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:49,061
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a metaphysical ignorance, or a mystery, or a secret behind the veil of maya, or something that you need to discern.

146
00:13:49,221 --> 00:13:49,381
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

147
00:13:49,982 --> 00:13:53,202
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your everyday attitude in regard to your everyday experience.

148
00:13:53,922 --> 00:14:04,744
[SPEAKER_00]: When you choose to ignore things that undermine your your gratuitous desire, your gratuitous ill-will, or your gratuitous need for distraction.

149
00:14:04,864 --> 00:14:05,224
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

150
00:14:05,584 --> 00:14:09,905
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop ignoring these things, you will, you will, you will automatically then,

151
00:14:11,477 --> 00:14:18,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Be removing layers and layers and layers of your lust, aversion and delusion.

152
00:14:18,702 --> 00:14:20,743
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

153
00:14:20,864 --> 00:14:21,804
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see ignoring.

154
00:14:21,844 --> 00:14:25,687
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not on the level of just being mindful every second of your life.

155
00:14:25,807 --> 00:14:27,348
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's on the level of your intentions.

156
00:14:28,889 --> 00:14:36,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intentions determine whether there is lust, whether you're acting out of lust or out of non-lust, absence of lust.

157
00:14:38,313 --> 00:14:44,599
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I say you practice mindfulness of these things, I don't mean you keep repeating to yourself, I'm present, I'm present, I'm sitting, I'm talking, I'm walking.

158
00:14:44,659 --> 00:14:44,839
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

159
00:14:45,720 --> 00:14:50,865
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain the knowledge of an enduring state of mind that determines your intention.

160
00:14:52,307 --> 00:14:53,428
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you don't ignore.

161
00:14:53,848 --> 00:14:56,271
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't ignore where your intentions are rooted.

162
00:14:56,991 --> 00:15:02,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Because by not ignoring where your intentions are rooted, you won't be able to act carelessly out of them.

163
00:15:03,485 --> 00:15:05,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Because action is a result of your intention.

164
00:15:06,107 --> 00:15:12,791
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're mindful of where your intention is rooted, you're, by default, mindful where your action would be rooted, if you were to perform it.

165
00:15:12,851 --> 00:15:15,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you abstain from it, if it's unwholesome.

166
00:15:16,574 --> 00:15:17,014
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

167
00:15:17,575 --> 00:15:26,961
[SPEAKER_00]: You stick to that fundamental principle and you would prevent your mind from gratuitous maintenance of lust, aversion and distraction.

168
00:15:27,501 --> 00:15:27,842
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

169
00:15:28,182 --> 00:15:31,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why that avijja is something you do.

170
00:15:32,367 --> 00:15:34,227
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something you don't know, you need to figure out.

171
00:15:34,728 --> 00:15:37,588
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's... well, okay, you cannot know what you're supposed to do.

172
00:15:37,648 --> 00:15:40,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, that also comes under vijā, but it's really more of an attitude.

173
00:15:42,229 --> 00:15:46,110
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain your vijā through the attitude of ignoring the nature of things.

174
00:15:47,211 --> 00:15:54,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, through acting out of sense of self, for example, you maintain the ignorance that your experience cannot belong to you.

175
00:15:55,413 --> 00:15:57,013
[SPEAKER_00]: You maintain the ignorance of anatta.

176
00:15:58,580 --> 00:16:00,061
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can see it's mutually exclusive.

177
00:16:00,422 --> 00:16:04,706
[SPEAKER_00]: By not acting out of sense of self, well, you're closer to understanding the nature of anatta.

178
00:16:07,088 --> 00:16:08,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't have it both ways.

179
00:16:08,609 --> 00:16:15,195
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be like, oh, I am not acting out of sense of self, but I still have a sense of self, that is mine, that belongs to me.

180
00:16:16,256 --> 00:16:17,057
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's either-or.

181
00:16:17,177 --> 00:16:21,401
[SPEAKER_00]: You're either with the grain of ignorance or against the grain of ignorance, if you stop ignoring it.

182
00:16:22,487 --> 00:16:34,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not incidental that, certainly, in the beginning, all the attitudes that go against the ignorance are pretty much the attitudes that will cause you lots of anxiety, lots of dread, lots of fear, lots of doubt, hindrances.

183
00:16:35,551 --> 00:16:37,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is the Mahārāja's defense.

184
00:16:38,092 --> 00:16:39,673
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't want you to stop ignoring.

185
00:16:39,893 --> 00:16:44,494
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how he keeps you bound to the experience that you don't really own.

186
00:16:46,015 --> 00:16:51,577
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, for most people, the spiritual practice just becomes the extension of their sensuality.

187
00:16:52,257 --> 00:17:02,246
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if your spiritual practice is not undoing your doubt, your anxiety, not through avoiding, but through being unaffected by it, so in the face of anxiety, you're not afraid.

188
00:17:02,266 --> 00:17:10,793
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you know, okay, well, this is certainly closer to the truth, than if I refuse to admit that I'm still liable to anxiety, for example.

189
00:17:10,833 --> 00:17:16,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And then my my practice is just finding sort of colorful distractions in the spiritual nature, disguised as spiritual things.

190
00:17:17,879 --> 00:17:19,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Meditation experiences,

191
00:17:20,128 --> 00:17:35,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Devotional, whatever else, practices, quite often, are fully rooted in just having something more meaningful to do, but you're equally depending on having something to do, which is what keeps you floating in that attitude of ignoring that prevents you from facing the truth of anxiety.

192
00:17:37,522 --> 00:17:38,383
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by undoing...

193
00:17:38,543 --> 00:17:39,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

194
00:17:39,224 --> 00:17:47,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by undoing your... By not maintaining your attitude of ignoring, you will be, certainly, initially, experiencing more anxiety, less confidence, more doubt,

195
00:17:48,964 --> 00:17:57,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're undoing non-doubt, confidence, absence of anxiety, that was based upon ignoring.

196
00:17:57,551 --> 00:17:59,633
[SPEAKER_00]: And that initial undoing would be unpleasant.

197
00:17:59,673 --> 00:18:13,604
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, once you go through with it, and you would go through with it, if your behavior has been developed, as in, if you were virtuous, not giving in to the gross acts of lust, aversion or distraction,

198
00:18:14,131 --> 00:18:29,869
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a form of strength, a form of container, that can then enable you to contain the very anxiety, or dread, or doubt, or whatever hindrance has been sort of stirred up on account of you facing the reality of things.

199
00:18:29,949 --> 00:18:31,691
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say reality, I mean the nature of them.

200
00:18:43,085 --> 00:18:49,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way, you want to start undermining, testing your sense of safety that you have now.

201
00:18:50,188 --> 00:19:03,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if it were truly true safety of being unable to suffer on account of anxiety, suffer on account of doubt, or suffer on account of hindrances, then it will stand the test.

202
00:19:05,254 --> 00:19:10,796
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's not that kind of safety, like safety of wisdom, safety of liberation,

203
00:19:12,018 --> 00:19:16,739
[SPEAKER_00]: You would be experiencing a degree of anxiety or fear, and that's the practice then.

204
00:19:17,919 --> 00:19:24,161
[SPEAKER_00]: It outlines the work for you perfectly.

205
00:19:24,181 --> 00:19:28,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, say, like practically now, say, you start questioning your sense of safety, and yeah, there's a bit of anxiety.

206
00:19:28,502 --> 00:19:30,302
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize, yeah, you could, you can, you can get sick.

207
00:19:30,342 --> 00:19:32,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody could come, burn your house, take you away, kill you, whatever.

208
00:19:35,482 --> 00:19:36,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are possibilities.

209
00:19:37,063 --> 00:19:40,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And you choose to dwell on them, not because of some sort of morbid fascination.

210
00:19:40,745 --> 00:19:46,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You choose to dwell on them because they counter your indolence and your complacency.

211
00:19:47,009 --> 00:19:51,432
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the automatic response if you don't question those things, if you don't think about those things.

212
00:19:53,033 --> 00:19:56,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you only do it for the practical purpose of this passion.

213
00:19:56,795 --> 00:19:59,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't be emotionally invested in something

214
00:19:59,914 --> 00:20:00,815
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, you know, 50-50.

215
00:20:00,915 --> 00:20:02,556
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be taken away.

216
00:20:03,136 --> 00:20:07,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, I better be careful how much I commit to it, because it might disappear the next moment.

217
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:11,922
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just ignore that fact, you start to assume, no, it's going to be here tomorrow.

218
00:20:11,962 --> 00:20:12,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

219
00:20:12,643 --> 00:20:13,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Just think positive.

220
00:20:13,643 --> 00:20:16,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Just think, it's going to be there forever, for as long as I need it.

221
00:20:16,805 --> 00:20:18,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yeah, of course, that's the attitude you're going to develop.

222
00:20:19,307 --> 00:20:27,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll be acting accordingly, as if, yeah, you are your own master, you're going to live forever, last forever, things will stay forever, as long as

223
00:20:29,094 --> 00:20:31,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And even people say, no, of course, we all know we're gonna die.

224
00:20:31,736 --> 00:20:33,937
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they still assume their sense of self will live forever.

225
00:20:35,238 --> 00:20:36,219
[SPEAKER_00]: That's very often the case.

226
00:20:37,099 --> 00:20:48,727
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be reflecting on these opposing themes, just to question that sense of gratuitous safety, that was based entirely on ignoring the possibility of these things.

227
00:20:48,747 --> 00:20:50,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, there's 50-50 with everything.

228
00:20:52,190 --> 00:20:54,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're gonna be alive or not tomorrow, it's 50-50.

229
00:20:55,912 --> 00:20:57,333
[SPEAKER_00]: But you choose to know, I'll probably be alive.

230
00:20:58,282 --> 00:20:59,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you choose that thought?

231
00:21:00,424 --> 00:21:01,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you might not be alive.

232
00:21:04,548 --> 00:21:05,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Death might be hanging over you.

233
00:21:05,689 --> 00:21:07,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it is actually hanging over all of us.

234
00:21:08,711 --> 00:21:10,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's certain, you just don't know exactly when.

235
00:21:10,973 --> 00:21:17,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you then keep investing your attention and choosing based upon as if you're gonna... you will see the old age?

236
00:21:17,839 --> 00:21:18,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

237
00:21:23,544 --> 00:21:25,326
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no justification for that.

238
00:21:25,828 --> 00:21:37,095
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you want to just practice mindfulness of that, not to the point of, oh, I want to die, but to the point of, okay, I'll practice enough, so I just stop gratuitously assuming that I will live for 50 years.

239
00:21:38,176 --> 00:21:39,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's 50-50.

240
00:21:39,196 --> 00:21:39,537
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

241
00:21:39,657 --> 00:21:40,857
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to push it further.

242
00:21:41,478 --> 00:21:43,779
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to prove yourself that you will die either.

243
00:21:44,300 --> 00:21:49,183
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to stay with that very unpleasant and kind of ambiguous 50-50.

244
00:21:51,969 --> 00:21:54,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you stay with it, that will become your theme.

245
00:21:55,552 --> 00:21:58,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you won't be able to act as if you're gonna live forever.

246
00:21:59,816 --> 00:22:13,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, already, a great deal of passion, anger, irritation, righteous indignation, need for distraction, it will be uprooted, because you remove the basis for it, basis for its growth.

247
00:22:14,921 --> 00:22:17,243
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how these contemplations are done rightly.

248
00:22:17,783 --> 00:22:23,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like develop like a full-on mental obsession of like, I'll die, I'll die, I'll die.

249
00:22:24,008 --> 00:22:24,168
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

250
00:22:24,188 --> 00:22:26,330
[SPEAKER_00]: That becoming equally mechanical, equally ignorant.

251
00:22:27,030 --> 00:22:29,692
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just trying to replace one thing with the other.

252
00:22:29,712 --> 00:22:29,852
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

253
00:22:29,892 --> 00:22:36,297
[SPEAKER_00]: You practice it only to the extent of putting in question what you've been taking for granted, not overdoing it.

254
00:22:37,598 --> 00:22:38,719
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's the middle way.

255
00:22:39,159 --> 00:22:42,982
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to discern that initially ambiguous ground,

256
00:22:45,055 --> 00:22:46,575
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be, it might not be.

257
00:22:46,595 --> 00:22:47,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, it won't be for me.

258
00:22:49,196 --> 00:22:50,516
[SPEAKER_00]: As some of the suttas say.

259
00:22:51,316 --> 00:22:53,297
[SPEAKER_00]: But not like, it will not be for me, I guarantee.

260
00:22:53,457 --> 00:22:53,577
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

261
00:22:53,757 --> 00:22:54,737
[SPEAKER_00]: It might, it might not.

262
00:22:55,357 --> 00:22:56,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, it's not for me.

263
00:22:57,758 --> 00:22:59,038
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I cannot own it.

264
00:23:00,238 --> 00:23:02,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I might live tomorrow, I might not live tomorrow.

265
00:23:03,159 --> 00:23:04,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, it's not mine.

266
00:23:05,359 --> 00:23:08,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were mine, I will know for sure that I will live tomorrow.

267
00:23:09,940 --> 00:23:10,440
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't.

268
00:23:13,437 --> 00:23:24,324
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that whole practice of, like, questioning the certainty of things is only so you remove the basis for acting out of passion towards the things.

269
00:23:24,364 --> 00:23:30,547
[SPEAKER_00]: And that basis is you must take it for granted that it is yours, that you are in its control, and it's going to be there for as long as you want it to be.

270
00:23:31,708 --> 00:23:35,610
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, if you have trouble grasping this theme, take something simpler.

271
00:23:37,352 --> 00:23:42,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, say there is, like a certain, I don't know, say undertaking.

272
00:23:43,456 --> 00:23:51,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, imagine somebody comes to you and asks you for... to invest a great amount of work, or finances, or whatever, into something.

273
00:23:52,704 --> 00:23:56,587
[SPEAKER_00]: They... well, they won't reward you, you know, in a great sense.

274
00:23:56,607 --> 00:23:59,890
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like a gamble, taking on risk to get more.

275
00:23:59,910 --> 00:24:01,952
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like, are you going to build this house?

276
00:24:02,432 --> 00:24:11,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you going to spend lots of time building, whereby you agree that at any point of your work investment, it will be torn down by somebody?

277
00:24:14,220 --> 00:24:18,563
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would consider, you would... well, if you have no choice, like you say you can't say no to it, you must do it.

278
00:24:19,243 --> 00:24:20,244
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you be doing it?

279
00:24:20,884 --> 00:24:28,568
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you be going about that undertaking, if fundamental sort of state of it, it's not up to you?

280
00:24:28,728 --> 00:24:37,233
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody can, in the middle of your work, in the middle of your commitment, in the middle of your investment, can just wipe it away.

281
00:24:37,253 --> 00:24:39,235
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have no choice, but you have to do it.

282
00:24:40,115 --> 00:24:41,936
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't say, okay, I'm not going to even start that.

283
00:24:42,076 --> 00:24:42,216
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

284
00:24:42,336 --> 00:24:43,177
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to start.

285
00:24:44,238 --> 00:24:50,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize you have no choice about doing things, but you certainly have a choice about how much you get invested in your doing.

286
00:24:51,582 --> 00:24:59,666
[SPEAKER_00]: How much passion you allow... how far you allow for passion to take you out there, in your project, in your fantasy of, I can't wait for this finished product.

287
00:24:59,786 --> 00:25:03,828
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, anything falls into this example I'm giving.

288
00:25:04,488 --> 00:25:10,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Starting a family, maintaining a family, ambition, work, study, even being a monk.

289
00:25:10,731 --> 00:25:13,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for many people would become like, oh, I'm going to develop this

290
00:25:14,329 --> 00:25:27,912
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, practicing great monastery and disciples, like, not necessarily crudely, but kind of implicitly, the monk's life takes you in this great, you know, whatever, ambitious undertaking of spreading dhamma.

291
00:25:29,752 --> 00:25:41,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if you keep yourself in check, in a sense that you realize that it doesn't matter how much you invest, it doesn't matter how much you value it, if it's taken away, you won't be able to prevent it.

292
00:25:42,848 --> 00:25:50,449
[SPEAKER_00]: That will automatically then, automatically, it will sort of dampen the amount of passion you can invest in it.

293
00:25:52,130 --> 00:25:56,190
[SPEAKER_00]: It will prevent you from sort of losing perspective on it.

294
00:25:56,811 --> 00:26:00,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And you maintain that, you can maintain that attitude in regard to everything, whatever you're about to do.

295
00:26:01,211 --> 00:26:05,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's a small thing, not a massive undertaking that takes years or something like that.

296
00:26:05,432 --> 00:26:06,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Just a smaller.

297
00:26:08,113 --> 00:26:09,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Day-to-day tasks.

298
00:26:09,793 --> 00:26:10,493
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't forget,

299
00:26:12,919 --> 00:26:31,996
[SPEAKER_00]: That your life can be taken away at any point of that invested effort in a certain task, means there won't be any passion to follow your effort of performing the task, which means you're already practicing a form of equanimity and indifference towards the task, because you don't lose the perspective of fundamental impermanence of it.

300
00:26:33,718 --> 00:26:34,058
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

301
00:26:34,318 --> 00:26:36,800
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to keep saying to yourself, oh, it's gonna be taken away, it's gonna be taken away.

302
00:26:37,501 --> 00:26:38,122
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do

303
00:26:39,188 --> 00:26:42,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't forget that it could be taken away.

304
00:26:42,150 --> 00:26:42,510
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

305
00:26:42,710 --> 00:26:43,551
[SPEAKER_00]: That's more than enough.

306
00:26:44,291 --> 00:26:51,196
[SPEAKER_00]: And you clarify that theme, you clarify... you fortify the basis for non-passion, non-aversion and non-distraction.

307
00:26:58,761 --> 00:27:01,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... comes back to that sense of safety.

308
00:27:01,843 --> 00:27:05,425
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel safety when you ignore the risks of your undertaking.

309
00:27:07,749 --> 00:27:09,670
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what your safety is based upon.

310
00:27:09,710 --> 00:27:19,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, most people's safety is based upon ignorance, of the possibility of being it otherwise, which is too unpleasant to dwell on.

311
00:27:21,834 --> 00:27:26,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why getting wisdom and developing mental strength goes hand in hand.

312
00:27:26,836 --> 00:27:32,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Mental strength, as in samādhi, being able to maintain the composure, goes hand in hand.

313
00:27:33,038 --> 00:27:37,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you're not able to maintain the composure of a certain thing,

314
00:27:37,632 --> 00:27:43,854
[SPEAKER_00]: The slightest sight of an act... a thing that might cause anxiety, you'll be looking away.

315
00:27:43,894 --> 00:27:44,575
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a problem.

316
00:27:44,795 --> 00:27:51,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you start practicing sense-restraint, verbal-restraint, so that then you can actually have a mental-restraint of running away.

317
00:27:51,697 --> 00:28:00,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Like... as the Buddha said, like the... the weak... the weak horse or the elephant that... that gets scared even just by the... by the sound of the battle.

318
00:28:00,261 --> 00:28:03,362
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't even see it, let alone gets into the fight.

319
00:28:04,047 --> 00:28:09,069
[SPEAKER_00]: He's already running away, lagging it, just at the sound of the enemy's drums.

320
00:28:11,410 --> 00:28:12,230
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not a given.

321
00:28:12,290 --> 00:28:14,111
[SPEAKER_00]: You are responsible for choosing to run away.

322
00:28:14,371 --> 00:28:22,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you run away at the sound of the enemy's drums, okay, start exposing yourself to the sound of enemy drums and withhold the tendency to run away.

323
00:28:22,914 --> 00:28:24,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Take it on a bit, bit by bit.

324
00:28:25,615 --> 00:28:26,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you might be able to take it on.

325
00:28:26,655 --> 00:28:29,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you run away at the sight of the drums, at the sight of the enemy.

326
00:28:30,377 --> 00:28:31,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you work with that.

327
00:28:31,834 --> 00:28:34,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, in the end, you'll be in the battle and you won't be running away.

328
00:28:36,297 --> 00:28:37,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not always a battle.

329
00:28:37,638 --> 00:28:40,179
[SPEAKER_00]: But, when it does come, you don't run away.

330
00:28:40,519 --> 00:28:41,040
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

331
00:28:41,460 --> 00:28:44,642
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't run away if your behavior is based on sīla and virtue.

332
00:28:45,882 --> 00:28:47,003
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're developing it rightly.

333
00:28:47,023 --> 00:28:48,364
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're practicing sense restraint.

334
00:28:49,805 --> 00:28:53,327
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be leaking that container that I mentioned earlier.

335
00:28:55,830 --> 00:29:02,674
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how your sense of safety will then not depend on presence or absence of enemy, presence or absence of threat.

336
00:29:03,335 --> 00:29:03,475
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

337
00:29:03,855 --> 00:29:07,698
[SPEAKER_00]: It will basically depend on your understanding of nature of things.

338
00:29:07,718 --> 00:29:11,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, this is not mine, so why would I need to be all anxious about protecting it?

339
00:29:13,441 --> 00:29:15,182
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything, your senses are not yours.

340
00:29:15,763 --> 00:29:20,926
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you can say, our senses are not mine, but I keep acting out of the sense of self, sense of ownership of the senses.

341
00:29:21,953 --> 00:29:22,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can say all you want.

342
00:29:23,334 --> 00:29:24,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes, everything is impermanent.

343
00:29:24,595 --> 00:29:25,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Modern science has proven the impermanence.

344
00:29:26,016 --> 00:29:26,956
[SPEAKER_00]: We all understand impermanence.

345
00:29:26,976 --> 00:29:27,516
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha was right.

346
00:29:27,957 --> 00:29:33,300
[SPEAKER_00]: You still act out of your sense of appropriation, which means what you're saying are just empty hollow phrases.

347
00:29:34,921 --> 00:29:38,723
[SPEAKER_00]: If you truly understand impermanence, you're impenetrable to fear and anxiety.

348
00:29:39,784 --> 00:29:43,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Because things that are liable to be destroyed are not your concern.

349
00:29:44,427 --> 00:29:45,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like that...

350
00:29:45,848 --> 00:29:47,909
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't remember the sutta off the top of my... Well, I remember the sutta.

351
00:29:47,929 --> 00:29:48,769
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know which one it was.

352
00:29:50,010 --> 00:29:50,711
[SPEAKER_00]: It was the...

353
00:29:52,066 --> 00:29:54,788
[SPEAKER_00]: That monk who was going around the forest saying, what a joy, what a delight.

354
00:29:55,369 --> 00:29:59,572
[SPEAKER_00]: And people heard him, other monks heard him, and they thought, oh, he must be thinking about his lay life.

355
00:30:00,933 --> 00:30:03,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And he's saying what a joy and delight there was.

356
00:30:03,175 --> 00:30:04,256
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not happy being a monk.

357
00:30:04,296 --> 00:30:05,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they went and told the Buddha.

358
00:30:05,217 --> 00:30:07,659
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha called him and said, is it true that you're saying this?

359
00:30:07,679 --> 00:30:08,420
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, yes, it's true.

360
00:30:08,460 --> 00:30:09,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why is that?

361
00:30:09,200 --> 00:30:10,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it because you think about lay life?

362
00:30:10,261 --> 00:30:11,622
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, no, no, no, no, quite the opposite.

363
00:30:12,023 --> 00:30:13,484
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, when I used to be a layman, I was a

364
00:30:16,995 --> 00:30:17,455
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

365
00:30:17,735 --> 00:30:20,496
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't have a moment of peace, because I was always fearing for my life.

366
00:30:20,677 --> 00:30:30,941
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I had guards guarding my palaces, guarding my doors, guarding me a day, guarding me a night, because it was a good likelihood somebody is gonna make an attempt in my life to take my place in line for the throne.

367
00:30:31,721 --> 00:30:35,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was living in constant fear, constant anxiety, constant defense.

368
00:30:36,505 --> 00:30:42,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Of things that I deemed mine and things that I deemed I'm entitled to, the throne and all the value and stuff that comes with it.

369
00:30:43,187 --> 00:30:51,870
[SPEAKER_00]: But then he gave it all up, became a monk, became an arahant, and he said that now I'm living in this completely wild forest with wild animals, tigers and elephants and snakes.

370
00:30:52,910 --> 00:30:59,072
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a good likelihood I might lose my life quickly, yet I have zero fear, because I understood the dhamma.

371
00:30:59,132 --> 00:31:02,933
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, he he stopped protecting things that are unprotectable.

372
00:31:03,746 --> 00:31:06,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Your life, your senses, your existence.

373
00:31:06,727 --> 00:31:09,628
[SPEAKER_00]: He gave it up and he said it's a tremendous joy.

374
00:31:09,688 --> 00:31:11,848
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he goes around the forest saying, what a joy, what a joy.

375
00:31:12,208 --> 00:31:23,011
[SPEAKER_00]: How much I used to defend, invest in self-defense through all sorts of gods and everything else and I was so unhappy and now I'm liable to die next moment and I experience complete happiness because there's nothing for me to defend.

376
00:31:23,292 --> 00:31:23,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Never was.

377
00:31:26,292 --> 00:31:28,253
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much all to it.

